{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OSS consensus audit (2026-08-13): APPROVED pattern.\n\nCPython's real subprocess tests use very short trigger timeouts (for example 0.05s/0.1s) to enter timeout paths, then a generous LONG_TIMEOUT only for cleanup/resume, avoiding a brittle upper-bound pass assertion (python/cpython@dd1194e, Lib/test/test_subprocess.py#L1150-L1160 and #L1496-L1504). urllib3 similarly uses millisecond-scale test-only pool/read/connect timeouts while keeping real socket paths (urllib3/urllib3@52dfb19, test/with_dummyserver/test_connectionpool.py#L751-L812; test/with_dummyserver/test_https.py#L592).\n\nThe proposed smaller injected stall/timeout/grace/drain values with polling, real process/socket execution, and unchanged production defaults match that practice. Tracker cross-check: suave-constant-cow requires a full throughput window and fizzy-trunk-salt requires bounded group reap/retry count; neither locks production-scale seconds into the tests. No rejection found."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786638519589477001,"uuid":"3ba1ae9d-a965-468b-a8d3-a4c005b6fc5d"}